<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Candarids</h1><p class="st-meta">1291 CE – 1461 CE · capital Kastamonu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A long-lived Turkish beylik of the Black Sea region, ruling Kastamonu and Sinop until the Ottomans took it in 1461.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Shams al-Din Yaman Candar, a Turkish commander in Ilkhanid service, was granted the district of Eflani in the 1290s. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleyman-pasha-candarid">Suleyman Pasha</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kastamonu">Kastamonu</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chobanids-of-kastamonu">Chobanids of Kastamonu</a> in 1309 and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sinope">Sinop</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pervaneids">Pervaneids</a> around 1322.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kure-kastamonu">Kure</a> and the port of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sinope">Sinop</a>, which traded with Genoese colonies across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kastamonu">Kastamonu</a> in the 1330s. The rulers patronised Turkish literature and built mosques in Kastamonu. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfendiyar-bey">Isfendiyar Bey</a>, after whom the family was also called, they survived the Ottoman advance by alliances and ceding territory.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kastamonu">Kastamonu</a> in 1392, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> restored the family in 1402. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sinope">Sinop</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-bey-of-candar">Ismail Bey</a> in 1461 on the way to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Trebizond</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/candarids.jpg" alt="Candarids" width="320" height="173"><figcaption>Image: self- based on WP locator maps · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Candaro%C4%9Fullar%C4%B1.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1291 CE – 1461 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kastamonu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Suleyman Pasha, Isfendiyar Bey, Ismail Bey</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jandarids, Isfendiyarids, Candaroğulları</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1376&amp;focus=candarids" aria-label="Candarids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5504 702 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Candarids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5824 958L5824 958M5824 958L6134 1224M5824 958L5709 970M5824 958L5854 938"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5824 958h0M6134 1224h0M5709 970h0M5854 938h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5824 958h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5824 958h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kastamonu. <a href="/atlas?year=1376&amp;focus=candarids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
